  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: An upper level ridge exiting the region\nwill leave zonal flow aloft, keeping the weather relatively calm \nthrough early next week. Temperatures this weekend will remain above \nnormal for this time of year, with highs in the upper 60s/lower 70s. \nMinimum temperatures will climb into the upper 30s/low 40s by early \nnext week. \n\nIf you're looking for a chance to go outside and enjoy the\noutdoors, the best chances for this are this weekend, as looking\ninto next week we start to see influences of the polar jet.\nStarting Tuesday afternoon, the base of an upper level trough will \nextend into southern California, bringing plenty of pacific moisture \nand lift to our region. This will result in chances for \nprecipitation for the remainder of the forecast period, with the \nhighest chances on Thursday. Additionally, there is a low chance of \nisolated thunderstorms on Tuesday. In terms of precipitation types, \ntemperatures remain high keeping it mostly rain, however some \nmountain peaks will see the potential for some overnight snow. As a \nresult of precipitation and increased cloud cover, high temperatures \nwill drop to the upper 50s and low 60s, while low temperatures will \ndrop to the mid 30s. Models continue to push back the timing of a \ncold front into late next week, continuing to give low confidence on \ntiming and intensity at this time. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence in the forecast\nthrough Monday. Medium confidence in the precipitation and\ntemperature change next week. Low confidence on the chances of a\ncold front late next week.",
